window.CASESETS = [
{ id:'cs1', title:'ChaiCraft: from one cart to 40 outlets', topics:['business','polc','mintz','forms'],
  text:`Rohan started ChaiCraft as a single tea cart outside a Chandigarh college in 2023, investing his own ₹2 lakh. By 2026 it has 40 outlets across Punjab and Haryana and 220 employees. Outlet managers complain that Rohan still approves every purchase himself, that different outlets make tea differently, and that nobody tracks wastage. Rohan spends his mornings visiting outlets and his evenings at business networking events, and recently represented ChaiCraft on a TV show about startups. A venture capital fund has offered ₹15 crore for 30% equity, but its due diligence requires ChaiCraft to have a separate legal identity and limited liability. Rohan's friend Simran, an operations expert, has offered to join as co-founder.`,
  qs:[
   ['Rohan currently runs ChaiCraft alone with his own money and unlimited liability. Its current form is most likely:','Sole proprietorship',['Private limited company','LLP','Cooperative'],'One owner, own capital and unlimited liability.'],
   ['To accept the VC offer, the most suitable form to convert to is:','Private limited company',['Partnership','Remaining a sole proprietorship','Cooperative society'],'VCs invest through equity shares; a private company gives separate identity, limited liability and the ability to issue shares with transfer restrictions.'],
   ['"Different outlets make tea differently" and "nobody tracks wastage" mainly show gaps in which functions?','Organising (standard procedures) and controlling (measuring performance)',['Leading and staffing only','Planning the menu only','Marketing and finance'],'Standardisation of procedures is organising; tracking wastage against standards is controlling.'],
   ['Rohan appearing on a TV show to talk about ChaiCraft is which Mintzberg role?','Spokesperson',['Figurehead','Disseminator','Liaison'],'Representing the organisation to outsiders and transmitting information = spokesperson.'],
   ['Rohan approving every purchase himself across 40 outlets is best fixed by:','Delegating authority to outlet managers with clear purchase limits and reporting',['Making employees work harder','Closing half the outlets','Asking the VC to approve purchases'],'Growth requires delegation and clear authority; this is the organising lesson from Healthy Bites.']
  ]},
{ id:'cs2', title:'Lumio\'s lighting experiment', topics:['hawthorne','evolution','comp'],
  text:`Lumio Electronics assembles LED bulbs in Ludhiana. The new plant head, inspired by an engineering consultant, timed every assembly step and redesigned tasks into small standard steps. Output rose 8%. To test further, she brightened lighting on Line A: output rose. She then dimmed it back: output rose again. Meanwhile Line C was offered a ₹2-per-bulb bonus, but output stayed flat; workers there said "we do 900 a day, no more, no less". An HR intern interviewed 300 workers and found most simply wanted supervisors to listen to their ideas.`,
  qs:[
   ['Timing each step and splitting work into standard tasks reflects:','Scientific management',['Human relations','Systems theory','Agile management'],'Taylor\'s approach of finding the most efficient way.'],
   ['Output on Line A rose both when lighting increased and decreased. The best explanation is:','The Hawthorne effect: workers responded to being observed and given attention',['Lighting has no effect on anything','Workers prefer dim light','The bonus on Line C spilled over'],'This mirrors the illumination experiment.'],
   ['Line C\'s flat output despite the bonus mirrors which Hawthorne experiment?','Bank wiring observation room',['Illumination study','Relay assembly test room','Mica-splitting test'],'An informal group norm overrode financial incentives.'],
   ['The ₹2-per-bulb bonus is a type of:','Piece-rate financial incentive',['Fringe benefit','Dearness allowance','Non-financial incentive'],'Pay per unit produced over and above regular pay.'],
   ['Based on the interviews, the best next step is:','Train supervisors to listen, involve workers in improvement ideas and recognise contributions',['Install brighter lights everywhere','Double the bonus for Line C','Remove all rest breaks'],'Hawthorne shows attention, communication and recognition drive productivity.']
  ]},
{ id:'cs3', title:'BrewBox enters the Indian coffee market', topics:['env','porter5','swot'],
  text:`BrewBox, a European capsule-coffee brand, plans to enter India. India's rules require foreign single-brand retailers to source part of their products locally. Coffee consumption among urban Gen-Z is rising fast, but average spending power is lower than in Europe. Capsule machines need imported parts from only two global manufacturers. Indian consumers can easily switch to instant coffee, filter coffee or café chains. Five large café chains already compete aggressively on price and loyalty programmes. New plastic-waste rules are making single-use capsules controversial. BrewBox has a strong global brand and patented capsule technology but no Indian supply chain.`,
  qs:[
   ['The local-sourcing requirement for foreign retailers is which PESTEL factor (as classified in the IKEA case)?','Political',['Social','Technological','Economic'],'FDI/sourcing rules are government policy decisions.'],
   ['Only two global manufacturers make the machine parts. This means:','Supplier bargaining power is high',['Buyer power is high','Threat of new entrants is high','Rivalry is low'],'Few suppliers with unique inputs hold strong power.'],
   ['Instant coffee and filter coffee represent:','Threat of substitutes',['Supplier power','Threat of new entrants','Bargaining power of suppliers'],'Different products meeting the same need.'],
   ['BrewBox\'s patented capsule technology is a ___, while plastic-waste rules are a ___.','Strength; threat',['Opportunity; weakness','Weakness; opportunity','Threat; strength'],'Patents are internal and positive; regulations are external and harmful.'],
   ['Using its strong global brand to tap rising Gen-Z coffee demand is which TOWS strategy?','SO',['WT','WO','ST'],'Strength + Opportunity.']
  ]},
{ id:'cs4', title:'Kavya Group\'s portfolio review', topics:['strategy','generic'],
  text:`Kavya Group operates five businesses: (1) a fast-growing EV-charging business, (2) a mature and stable packaged-water brand, (3) a textile unit that has made losses for three years but has a strong brand that could recover with new management, (4) a small, non-core printing press that distracts management, and (5) a hardware store chain that has been loss-making for seven years with no hope of recovery. The board also wants the EV-charging unit to make its own charging cables instead of buying them, and the water brand to start selling directly to customers through its own app.`,
  qs:[
   ['Investing heavily to grow the EV-charging business is:','Expansion',['Stability','Turnaround','Liquidation'],'Growing scope = expansion.'],
   ['Bringing new management and cutting costs at the textile unit is:','Turnaround',['Divestment','Liquidation','Stability'],'Fix it.'],
   ['Selling the non-core printing press is:','Divestment',['Liquidation','Turnaround','Concentration'],'Sell it.'],
   ['The EV unit making its own charging cables is:','Backward integration',['Forward integration','Unrelated diversification','Stability'],'Moving toward suppliers.'],
   ['Using all these different strategies across the group at once is called:','Combination strategy',['Pure expansion','Stability','Blue ocean'],'Different strategies for different businesses.']
  ]},
{ id:'cs5', title:'Two coffee shops on one street', topics:['generic','rbv'],
  text:`On the same street in Mohali, "QuickCup" sells standard coffee at ₹60, serves 900 customers a day using a self-order kiosk, buys beans in bulk and keeps staff costs low. Opposite it, "Origin Roasters" charges ₹280 for single-origin coffee, roasts its own beans, offers baristas who explain each brew, and has a cosy reading room. Origin's head roaster has 15 years of experience and a secret roasting process no competitor has replicated, and the shop's processes are designed around this expertise. A third café, "PawBrew", serves only pet owners and lets them bring dogs, with special pet treats.`,
  qs:[
   ['QuickCup\'s generic strategy is:','Cost leadership',['Differentiation','Differentiation focus','Cost focus'],'Low cost, high volume, standardisation, broad market.'],
   ['Origin Roasters\' generic strategy is:','Differentiation',['Cost leadership','Cost focus','Stability'],'Unique experience and quality, premium price.'],
   ['PawBrew\'s strategy is best described as:','Differentiation focus (needs-based niche)',['Cost leadership','Unrelated diversification','Red ocean cost cutting'],'A narrow segment served with specialised value.'],
   ['Origin\'s head roaster\'s secret process passes all VRIO tests. This means Origin has:','Sustained competitive advantage',['Competitive parity','Temporary advantage','Competitive disadvantage'],'Valuable, rare, inimitable and organised.'],
   ['Origin roasting its own beans instead of buying roasted beans is:','Backward integration',['Forward integration','Divestment','Retrenchment'],'Moving toward supply.']
  ]},
{ id:'cs6', title:'Speedmart\'s Diwali stock chaos', topics:['scm','ops','wcm'],
  text:`Speedmart, a quick-commerce app in Delhi, promises 15-minute delivery. Before Diwali, customer demand for dry fruits rose 6%, but Speedmart\'s dark stores ordered 15% more, distributors ordered 30% more and the processor increased production by 45%. After Diwali, warehouses were overflowing and ₹40 lakh was stuck in unsold stock. Meanwhile, customers complained that orders arrived in 40 minutes because riders were short, and 8% of orders had wrong items, leading to refunds. Each dark store uses 50 kg of almonds daily and the supplier takes 4 days to deliver.`,
  qs:[
   ['The growing order swings from dark stores to the processor show:','The bullwhip effect',['The Hawthorne effect','Economies of scale','Central tendency'],'Small demand changes amplified upstream.'],
   ['Wrong items reaching customers and causing refunds are costs of:','External failure',['Internal failure','Prevention','Appraisal'],'Defects found after reaching the customer.'],
   ['Promising 15 minutes but delivering in 40 because of rider shortage shows:','Poor operations–strategy linkage',['Strong alignment','Good TQM','Effective forward integration'],'Marketing promises not backed by capacity.'],
   ['The reorder level for almonds per dark store is:','200 kg',['54 kg','12.5 kg','46 kg'],'Usage × lead time = 50 × 4.'],
   ['The ₹40 lakh stuck in unsold stock mainly affects the firm\'s:','Working capital (money blocked in inventory)',['Capital structure','Brand values','Vision statement'],'Excess inventory blocks working capital.']
  ]},
{ id:'cs7', title:'Ratios at Sunrise Traders', topics:['ratios','wcm','fsa'],
  text:`Sunrise Traders\' latest balance sheet shows: current assets ₹12 lakh (including stock ₹7 lakh and prepaid expenses ₹1 lakh), current liabilities ₹4 lakh, long-term loans ₹18 lakh, shareholders\' funds ₹12 lakh. Net sales were ₹60 lakh and gross profit ₹12 lakh. Last year net sales were ₹48 lakh. The owner is proud that the current ratio is "very high" and wants another ₹10 lakh bank loan to open a new store.`,
  qs:[
   ['Sunrise\'s current ratio is:','3:1',['2:1','1:3','4:1'],'12 ÷ 4.'],
   ['Its quick ratio is:','1:1',['3:1','2:1','0.75:1'],'Liquid assets = 12 − (7 + 1) = 4; 4 ÷ 4 = 1:1.'],
   ['The big gap between current and quick ratio suggests:','A large share of current assets is tied up in stock',['Excess cash','Very low inventory','No liabilities'],'Stock is excluded from quick assets.'],
   ['The debt-equity ratio is:','1.5:1',['0.67:1','2:1','3:1'],'18 ÷ 12 = 1.5.'],
   ['Sales growth over last year (trend analysis) is:','25%',['20%','12%','80%'],'(60 − 48) ÷ 48 × 100 = 25%.']
  ]},
{ id:'cs8', title:'Novatech\'s hiring rush', topics:['recruit','hrm','training'],
  text:`Novatech, a Bengaluru SaaS firm, needs 300 graduate engineers in two months, a confidential replacement for its CFO, and 40 customer-support executives. HR currently spends most of its time on payroll and attendance. The CEO wants HR to become a strategic partner. New joiners will need to learn the company\'s internal software, and the support team\'s average resolution score is 3.1 against a target of 4.0.`,
  qs:[
   ['Best method for the 300 graduate engineers:','Campus recruitment (direct method)',['Executive search firm','Labour contractor','Newspaper ad only'],'Large, fast, employer-branding hire.'],
   ['Best method for the confidential CFO replacement:','Executive search firm (third-party method)',['Walk-in interview','Campus drive','Open job fair'],'Confidential and specialised.'],
   ['HR spending most of its time on payroll and attendance reflects which approach?','Personnel management',['Strategic HRM','HR analytics','Change agent role'],'Administrative, reactive HR.'],
   ['The gap between the 3.1 resolution score and the 4.0 target is used to identify:','Training needs (gap concept)',['Recruitment sources','Capital structure','Generic strategy'],'Gap between actual and expected performance.'],
   ['Teaching new joiners the internal software while they work on live tickets with a senior buddy is:','On-the-job training',['Off-the-job training','Education','Development'],'Learning while performing actual job tasks.']
  ]},
{ id:'cs9', title:'Appraisal season at Meridian Bank', topics:['appraisal','comp'],
  text:`At Meridian Bank, branch manager Mr. Bedi rated all 15 officers "3 – average". Ms. Rao, another manager, gave her whole team "5 – outstanding". An officer who was excellent all year got a low rating after a mistake in the final week. Officers complain that ratings feel subjective. HR proposes a new system where each rating point is linked to a specific observable behaviour, plus a process where officers get feedback from peers, customers and juniors, receive coaching, and then get the same feedback again six months later. Officers also want their pay to reflect performance.`,
  qs:[
   ['Mr. Bedi\'s ratings show:','Central tendency error',['Leniency error','Halo effect','Recency error'],'Everyone rated average.'],
   ['Ms. Rao\'s ratings show:','Leniency error',['Strictness error','Central tendency','Similar-to-me'],'Unusually high ratings for all.'],
   ['The low rating after one final-week mistake is:','Recency error',['Halo effect','Central tendency','Leniency'],'Based on most recent behaviour.'],
   ['Linking each rating point to a specific observable behaviour is:','BARS',['Essay method','Forced distribution','Checklist'],'Behaviourally anchored rating scale.'],
   ['Multi-source feedback, then coaching, then repeat feedback is:','720° appraisal',['360° appraisal','MBO','Self-appraisal'],'Two feedback cycles with development in between.']
  ]},
{ id:'cs10', title:'GreenLeaf Foods\' financing decision', topics:['capstr','fsa'],
  text:`GreenLeaf Foods needs ₹50 crore to build a new plant. Option A: issue new equity shares to outside investors, which would reduce the founders\' stake from 70% to 45%. Option B: take a bank loan at 9% interest, which is tax-deductible. The CFO believes "moderate debt lowers our cost of capital, but beyond a point the risk and cost rise sharply". GreenLeaf\'s sales are seasonal and fell 30% during the last monsoon. In its cash flow statement, the plant purchase will be recorded separately from the loan received.`,
  qs:[
   ['A key advantage of Option B (debt) for the founders is:','No dilution of ownership and control, plus the interest tax shield',['No repayment obligation','Lenders share business risk equally','Fixed dividends to lenders'],'Debt keeps control and interest is tax-deductible.'],
   ['The CFO\'s belief matches which capital structure theory?','Traditional approach',['Net Operating Income approach','Modigliani–Miller approach','Baumol model'],'Optimum structure: moderate debt helps, excessive debt hurts.'],
   ['Given seasonal sales that fell 30%, the biggest risk of heavy debt is:','Fixed interest payments during low-sales periods magnify losses (financial risk)',['Loss of control to shareholders','Higher dividend payout','Lower tax shield'],'Leverage magnifies losses when sales drop.'],
   ['Buying the plant appears in the cash flow statement under:','Investing activities',['Financing activities','Operating activities','Not at all'],'Acquisition of long-term assets.'],
   ['The bank loan received appears under:','Financing activities',['Investing activities','Operating activities','Cash equivalents'],'Borrowings = financing.']
  ]},
{ id:'cs11', title:'FitFuel\'s protein bar problem', topics:['marketing','swot'],
  text:`FitFuel makes protein bars in Mohali. Its founders spent two years perfecting a bar with 30 g protein and 12 vitamins, believing "a better product will sell itself". Sales are weak. To fix this, the sales head started pushing distributors with heavy discounts and targets. A customer survey later showed buyers mainly want a tasty, affordable snack between college classes, not maximum protein. FitFuel has now decided to launch a ₹40 smaller bar in new flavours, sell it in college canteens and on quick-commerce apps, and run Instagram campaigns with student influencers. It will also switch to compostable wrappers.`,
  qs:[
   ['Spending two years perfecting the bar, believing it would "sell itself", reflects:','Product concept',['Marketing concept','Societal marketing concept','Production concept'],'Focus on quality and features over customer needs.'],
   ['Ignoring that buyers wanted a tasty, affordable snack is an example of:','Marketing myopia',['Bullwhip effect','Central tendency','Hawthorne effect'],'Loving the product, losing sight of the need.'],
   ['Pushing distributors with heavy discounts and targets reflects:','Selling concept',['Holistic marketing','Marketing concept','Product concept'],'Hard-sell focus on volume and quotas.'],
   ['Selling in college canteens and on quick-commerce apps is a decision about which P?','Place',['Price','Promotion','Process'],'Distribution channels and coverage.'],
   ['Switching to compostable wrappers moves FitFuel towards:','Societal marketing concept',['Production concept','Selling concept','Product concept'],'Balancing profit, customer wants and environmental welfare.']
  ]}
];

/* ======= DESCRIPTIVE CASES (written answers with model answers) ======= */
window.CASES = [
{ id:'d1', title:'Healthy Bites: partnership, company or investor?', topics:['business','forms','polc'],
  text:`Aarav and Meera run Healthy Bites, a healthy-food outlet near a university, as a partnership. Daily customers grew from 60 to 250 and online orders started. Staff say nobody is clear about responsibilities and the kitchen gets instructions from both founders. Customers complain about late delivery, wrong orders and inconsistent quality. Aarav wants everyone to "work harder"; Meera wants better organisation and management. A large restaurant chain offers to buy 40% of the business to fund expansion across India.`,
  qs:[
   ['Diagnose the real problem at Healthy Bites using management functions.', `<p><b>Problem:</b> the business grew faster than its management system. Informal management worked with 4 employees, but more customers → more staff → more orders → more complexity.</p><ul><li><b>Planning gap:</b> no plan for the delivery process or targets.</li><li><b>Organising gap:</b> unclear responsibilities; kitchen staff get instructions from both founders (no unity of command).</li><li><b>Leading gap:</b> staff feel confused and unheard; Aarav's "work harder" ignores motivation.</li><li><b>Controlling gap:</b> no tracking of late or wrong orders and quality.</li></ul><p><b>Conclusion:</b> Meera is right. The issue is organisation and management, not only employee effort.</p>`],
   ['Recommend specific actions using POLC.', `<ul><li><b>Plan:</b> set delivery-time and quality targets (e.g., 95% orders in 30 min), plan delivery routes and peak-hour staffing.</li><li><b>Organise:</b> divide responsibilities (Aarav: purchasing & finance; Meera: operations & people), appoint a kitchen supervisor and a delivery lead, one reporting line per team, standard recipes and SOPs.</li><li><b>Lead:</b> train employees, take regular feedback, recognise good performance.</li><li><b>Control:</b> use order-tracking technology, track KPIs (late %, wrong-order %, ratings) weekly, and take corrective action.</li></ul>`],
   ['Should they stay a partnership, become a company, or accept the investor? Justify.', `<p>Compare on <b>capital, liability, control, continuity and growth</b>.</p><ul><li><b>Partnership:</b> simple and flexible, but limited capital and significant personal liability; not ideal for pan-India expansion.</li><li><b>Private limited company:</b> separate legal identity, limited liability, continuity, and ability to issue shares to investors. It suits growth ambitions.</li><li><b>Investor (40%):</b> brings capital and possibly expertise, but dilutes ownership and control; founders keep 60% so retain majority control.</li></ul><p><b>Recommendation:</b> convert to a private limited company first, fix the management system, then accept the investor on terms that protect founder control (board seats, clear roles). Expanding before fixing management would multiply current problems.</p>`]
  ]},
{ id:'d2', title:'Kodak: what should leadership have done?', topics:['swot','evolution','rbv'],
  text:`Kodak dominated photography for a century, with about 90% of the US film market. In 1975 its engineer Steven Sasson invented the first digital camera, but leadership feared it would cannibalise film, which produced over 70% of profits. Kodak delayed digital for nearly two decades while Canon, Sony and Fujifilm invested heavily. It filed for bankruptcy in January 2012.`,
  qs:[
   ['Prepare a SWOT analysis of Kodak around 1980.', `<ul><li><b>Strengths:</b> most trusted photography brand; global distribution; owned the digital camera patent; strong R&D.</li><li><b>Weaknesses:</b> 70%+ profit from film; slow, risk-averse, bureaucratic culture; incentives rewarded protecting film; leadership suppressed its own innovation.</li><li><b>Opportunities:</b> emerging, unclaimed digital photography market; first-mover technology lead; later, camera phones and image sensors.</li><li><b>Threats:</b> competitors investing in digital; film becoming obsolete; consumers moving to screen-based sharing.</li></ul>`],
   ['Convert the SWOT into TOWS strategies.', `<ul><li><b>SO:</b> use the brand and R&D to lead digital imaging.</li><li><b>WO:</b> use digital growth to reduce dependence on film.</li><li><b>ST:</b> use brand, distribution and technology to compete with Sony, Canon, Fujifilm.</li><li><b>WT:</b> restructure the film business and accelerate digital transformation.</li></ul><p>SWOT helped understand the situation; TOWS converts it into action, which Kodak failed to do.</p>`],
   ['What management lessons does Kodak teach?', `<ul><li>Protecting today's profits can destroy tomorrow's business: be willing to cannibalise yourself.</li><li>Values and incentives must reward innovation, not only existing sales.</li><li>The environment is dynamic; continuous environmental scanning is essential.</li><li>Modern management (agility, digital transformation) is about continuously adapting to create value.</li><li>Having a valuable, rare resource (the patent) is useless if the firm isn't <b>organised</b> to exploit it (the O in VRIO).</li></ul>`]
  ]},
{ id:'d3', title:'IKEA\'s 12-year road to India', topics:['env'],
  text:`IKEA wanted to enter India in the early 2000s but opened its first store in Hyderabad only in 2018. It had to source 30% of products locally, redesign products starting at ₹199, offer paid assembly and home delivery, research over 1,000 Indian homes, launch online ordering early, source sustainable wood and comply with labour, leasing and GST laws.`,
  qs:[
   ['Analyse IKEA\'s entry using PESTEL.', `<ul><li><b>Political:</b> FDI rules required 30% local sourcing; IKEA restructured its supply chain.</li><li><b>Economic:</b> lower spending power; products from ₹199.</li><li><b>Social:</b> smaller homes, joint families, carpenter culture → paid assembly and delivery, Indian food in stores, re-shot ads; research of 1,000+ homes.</li><li><b>Technological:</b> fast smartphone adoption → online and app shopping from day one.</li><li><b>Environmental:</b> sustainable wood; clearances for large stores; smaller city-store format.</li><li><b>Legal:</b> labour, real-estate leasing and GST compliance.</li></ul>`],
   ['What does the IKEA case teach about environmental scanning and the contingency approach?', `<p>IKEA's long delay shows <b>environmental scanning</b> (scan → analyse → interpret → act) is essential before entering a new market. Its global model didn't work as-is; it adapted product, price, service and store format. This is <b>contingency thinking</b>: "what works in Sweden may not work in India". Its India-first small city-store format is now tested in other Asian markets, showing adaptation can become an advantage.</p>`]
  ]},
{ id:'d4', title:'QuickBite\'s quick-commerce promise', topics:['scm','ops'],
  text:`QuickBite, a grocery app, advertises "groceries in 10 minutes". Orders rose 40% in three months. Customers now complain about late deliveries, missing items and app crashes. Marketing wants to expand to two new cities. Operations says dark stores are short of inventory and riders, and demand forecasting is done manually. Suppliers complain that QuickBite\'s orders swing wildly week to week.`,
  qs:[
   ['Identify the operational problems and link them to concepts.', `<ul><li><b>Poor operations–strategy linkage:</b> marketing promises 10 minutes but operations lacks inventory, riders and capacity.</li><li><b>Bullwhip effect:</b> manual forecasting and no information sharing cause wild order swings upstream.</li><li><b>Quality failure (external):</b> missing items and late deliveries cause refunds, complaints and reputational damage.</li><li><b>Systems theory:</b> app crashes affect orders, customers and sales across the whole system.</li></ul>`],
   ['Recommend an operations strategy for QuickBite.', `<ul><li>Align on the competitive priority (<b>time + dependability</b>) before expanding; delay new cities until capacity matches promises.</li><li>Add local inventory in dark stores and rider capacity for peak hours.</li><li>Use AI demand forecasting and share real-time data with suppliers to reduce the bullwhip effect.</li><li>Quality: pick-and-pack checks, barcode scanning (prevent internal failures from becoming external).</li><li>Continuous improvement: track delivery time, accuracy and app uptime as KPIs.</li></ul><p>Corporate strategy → market needs → operations design → competitive advantage.</p>`]
  ]},
{ id:'d5', title:'StyleKart\'s working capital squeeze', topics:['wcm','ratios'],
  text:`StyleKart, an online fashion retailer, recently offered customers 90-day credit (earlier 15 days) to boost sales. It also stocks large inventories six months ahead of each season. Suppliers have reduced their credit from 60 days to 15 days. Despite record sales, StyleKart is struggling to pay salaries. Current assets are ₹80 lakh (inventory ₹55 lakh), current liabilities ₹40 lakh.`,
  qs:[
   ['Why is a firm with record sales struggling to pay salaries?', `<p>Its <b>working capital</b> is blocked. Working capital = CA − CL = 80 − 40 = ₹40 lakh, but most of it is in inventory and receivables, not cash.</p><ul><li><b>Liberal credit policy</b> (90 days) → money stuck in receivables.</li><li><b>High inventory policy</b> → ₹55 lakh blocked in stock, obsolescence risk in fashion.</li><li><b>Less supplier credit</b> (60 → 15 days) → pays suppliers faster.</li><li>Current ratio 2:1 looks fine, but <b>quick ratio = (80 − 55) ÷ 40 = 0.625:1</b>, below the 1:1 norm: poor liquidity.</li></ul>`],
   ['Suggest working capital tools and actions.', `<ul><li>Tighten credit policy (e.g., 30 days) and improve collection.</li><li>Use <b>EOQ</b> to decide order sizes and <b>ABC analysis</b> to control high-value stock tightly.</li><li>Set minimum, maximum and <b>re-order levels</b> instead of stocking six months ahead.</li><li>Negotiate longer supplier credit.</li><li>Cash management: since fashion sales are uncertain, use a <b>Miller-Orr</b> style control limit with upper/lower cash limits.</li></ul>`]
  ]},
{ id:'d6', title:'TalentHub\'s appraisal revolt', topics:['appraisal','comp','hrm'],
  text:`At TalentHub, managers rate employees once a year. Employees say ratings depend on whether the manager likes them, that one manager rates everyone "average", and that recent mistakes outweigh a year of good work. Salary increments are linked to ratings, so attrition has risen to 22%. Pay is also lower than competitors and two employees in identical roles earn very different salaries.`,
  qs:[
   ['Identify the appraisal errors and compensation problems.', `<ul><li><b>Similar-to-me / popularity bias:</b> ratings depend on liking.</li><li><b>Central tendency:</b> everyone rated average.</li><li><b>Recency error:</b> recent mistakes outweigh the full year.</li><li><b>Internal equity</b> violated: unequal pay for identical roles.</li><li><b>External competitiveness</b> violated: pay below competitors → 22% attrition.</li></ul>`],
   ['Design a better appraisal and compensation system.', `<ul><li>Use <b>BARS</b> with behavioural anchors per dimension to reduce subjectivity.</li><li>Add <b>MBO</b> for measurable goals set jointly.</li><li>Use <b>360°</b> (or 720° with coaching) feedback for leadership roles.</li><li><b>Continuous feedback</b> and documented critical incidents instead of a single annual review (fixes recency).</li><li>Rater training on errors; use problem-solving interviews.</li><li>Job evaluation and salary benchmarking for internal equity and external competitiveness; link incentives to performance; add engagement measures.</li></ul>`]
  ]},
{ id:'d7', title:'Tata-style group strategy', topics:['strategy','generic','rbv'],
  text:`A diversified Indian group runs steel, hotels, IT services, EVs and a loss-making FMCG unit. The chairman has ₹1,000 crore to allocate. The EV business is growing fast and depends on imported batteries. The hotel unit is mature and profitable. The FMCG unit has lost money for five years and is non-core.`,
  qs:[
   ['Separate corporate-level from business-level decisions in this case.', `<p><b>Corporate level</b> (top management): which businesses to be in and how to allocate the ₹1,000 crore: e.g., invest in EVs, maintain hotels, exit FMCG. <b>Business level</b> (each SBU): how the EV unit competes against rivals (cost, technology, brand). <b>Functional</b>: HR hiring EV engineers, finance raising funds, operations building battery supply.</p>`],
   ['Recommend a combination strategy with reasons.', `<ul><li><b>EVs → expansion</b> with <b>backward integration</b> into batteries to reduce import dependence, control quality and cost.</li><li><b>Hotels → stability</b>: consolidate and improve gradually; use its cash to fund EVs.</li><li><b>FMCG → divestment</b> (sell it) since it is non-core and loss-making; liquidation only if no buyer exists.</li><li><b>IT services → related diversification</b> into EV software/charging tech for synergy.</li></ul><p>Corporate strategy = portfolio + resources + direction.</p>`]
  ]},
{ id:'d8', title:'GigRide and the future of work', topics:['hrtrends','comp','hrm'],
  text:`GigRide, a ride-hailing app, has 50,000 drivers paid per ride with no fixed salary. It uses AI to allocate rides and automatically deactivate low-rated drivers. Drivers complain of income insecurity, no health benefits and unfair algorithmic decisions. GigRide wants to expand to Southeast Asia and hire a regional leadership team there.`,
  qs:[
   ['Evaluate GigRide\'s HR model from the perspective of modern HR trends.', `<ul><li><b>Deregulation & gig economy:</b> flexible, low-cost, quick income for drivers, but job insecurity and low loyalty.</li><li><b>AI in HR:</b> efficient but risks bias, lack of human judgment and ethical concerns. "AI can shortlist, but humans must make final decisions."</li><li><b>Maintenance gap:</b> no welfare or health benefits → poor retention.</li><li>Balancing act: technology vs human touch, flexibility vs security.</li></ul>`],
   ['Recommend HR actions for fairness and global expansion.', `<ul><li>Human review and an appeal process before deactivation; regular audits of the algorithm for bias.</li><li>Fringe benefits: accident and health insurance; incentives for safe, high-rated driving (group/individual incentives).</li><li>Use HR analytics to spot burnout and attrition patterns.</li><li>For Southeast Asia: expatriate management, international recruitment, cross-cultural training and inclusive leadership.</li></ul>`]
  ]},
{ id:'d9', title:'Café Brew-Haus: marketing a service', topics:['marketing'],
  text:`Brew-Haus is a new café near a university. The owner says "our coffee is the best in the city, people will come". After three months, footfall is low. Reviews mention a 20-minute wait, confused ordering, untrained staff and a dull interior. A rival café across the road offers student combos, quick service and an Instagram-friendly space.`,
  qs:[
   ['Which marketing philosophy is the owner following, and what is the problem with it?', `<p>The owner follows the <b>product concept</b>: believing a superior product ("best coffee") will sell itself. This leads to <b>marketing myopia</b>: focusing on the product (the coffee) rather than the customer's real <b>needs</b> (quick, affordable, comfortable place to hang out between classes). He should move to the <b>marketing concept</b>: start from the target market's needs and deliver them better than the rival.</p>`],
   ['Recommend a 7P marketing mix for Brew-Haus.', `<ul><li><b>Product:</b> keep quality coffee, add quick snacks and student combos.</li><li><b>Price:</b> student-friendly combos; penetration pricing to win share from the rival.</li><li><b>Place:</b> stay near campus, add delivery through food apps.</li><li><b>Promotion:</b> Instagram, student influencers, loyalty card, launch offers.</li><li><b>People:</b> train baristas in speed and friendliness.</li><li><b>Process:</b> fix the 20-minute wait with QR/counter ordering and a simple menu.</li><li><b>Physical evidence:</b> brighter, Instagram-friendly interiors, seating, plug points, branded cups.</li></ul><p><b>Conclusion:</b> as a service, Brew-Haus must manage the experience (people, process, physical evidence), not just the coffee.</p>`]
  ]}
];
